WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.527
The analytical reasoning in this module transfers across SQL systems, but some syntax changes.

00:00:05.527 --> 00:00:11.282
PostgreSQL can subtract timestamps directly in ways that other databases may express with functions such as

00:00:11.282 --> 00:00:11.802
DATEDIFF.

00:00:11.802 --> 00:00:17.062
PostgreSQL FILTER is concise for conditional aggregation, while SUM with CASE is supported more broadly.

00:00:17.062 --> 00:00:22.392
ROLLUP, CUBE, GROUPING SETS, and window functions are widely available in modern databases,

00:00:22.392 --> 00:00:25.427
but exact syntax and supported options can vary.

00:00:25.427 --> 00:00:29.047
When moving a query between systems, preserve the reasoning first: filter source rows, define the grain,

00:00:29.047 --> 00:00:31.662
calculate measures, filter groups, and sort the report.

00:00:31.662 --> 00:00:34.877
Then adapt the engine-specific syntax.

00:00:34.877 --> 00:00:40.462
That approach is much safer than copying a PostgreSQL query and assuming every database interprets it exactly

00:00:40.462 --> 00:00:44.212
the same way. Connect this idea to a retail report comparing revenue across products and regions.

00:00:44.212 --> 00:00:49.572
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:49.572 --> 00:00:56.042
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:56.042 --> 00:00:59.572
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:59.572 --> 00:01:04.300
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
